<h2>Why the data has limits: reporting bias</h2>

<p>NOAA hail data is the best long-term public record available, but it is not a complete count of every hailstorm. A few things to keep in mind:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>More people, more reports.</strong> Hail is reported when someone sees it, measures it, and tells the National Weather Service. Storms over McAllen, Mission, and Edinburg are more likely to be reported than storms over ranchland in the northern part of the county.</li>
<li><strong>Older records are sparse.</strong> Reporting networks, radar, spotter programs, and social media have all expanded over the decades. Fewer reports in the 1950s through 1980s does not mean less hail fell.</li>
<li><strong>Reports, not storms.</strong> One storm can generate many reports, while another produces none. The monthly table shows when reports cluster, not the exact number of hail days.</li>
<li><strong>Size is often estimated.</strong> Many reports compare stones to objects instead of measuring them. The SPC encourages measurement over estimation for this reason.</li>
</ul>

<p>Even with those caveats, the seasonal pattern is consistent and lines up with what RGV roofers and adjusters see every spring.</p>

<h2>What size hail damages a roof?</h2>

<p>This is one of the most common questions we hear, and the honest answer is that there is no single threshold. Damage depends on several factors working together:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Roof age and condition.</strong> Older composition shingles that have lost flexibility and granules tend to be more vulnerable than newer ones.</li>
<li><strong>Material.</strong> Composition shingles, metal panels, tile, and low-slope commercial membranes all respond differently. Metal may dent without leaking, which can raise cosmetic-damage questions under some policies.</li>
<li><strong>Wind-driven angle.</strong> Hail driven by strong wind strikes harder and at an angle, and it tends to concentrate damage on the slopes facing the storm.</li>
<li><strong>Stone density and shape.</strong> Hard, dense stones can do more damage than slushy ones of the same size.</li>
</ul>

<p>As a general pattern, larger hail is more likely to cause functional damage, and golf ball size hail or larger often gets the attention of roofers and adjusters. But smaller hail combined with strong wind can still damage an aging roof, and large hail does not damage every roof it hits. A slope-by-slope inspection is the only way to know. Our article on <a href="/roof-hail-damage-insurance-claim-mcallen/">roof hail damage insurance claims in McAllen</a> covers what inspectors look for.</p>

<h2>Why a busy hail season can complicate your claim</h2>

<p>Because hail clusters so tightly in April and May, it is not unusual for the same neighborhood to see more than one hail event in a single spring, or several over a few years. The April 21, 2023 storm, for example, came only about a week before the severe wind event of April 28, 2023, which NWS storm surveys found produced estimated gusts near 85 mph in McAllen. When storms stack up like that, insurers may ask which event caused which damage, or argue that the damage came from a storm before your policy started.</p>

<p>You can make that question easier to answer with a few habits:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Photograph your roof and exterior each year</strong>, ideally before March, so you have a dated record of its condition going into hail season.</li>
<li><strong>Keep every roofing invoice and inspection report</strong>, including from when you bought the property.</li>
<li><strong>Report each storm separately</strong> if you believe each one caused new damage, and note the date you first noticed it.</li>
<li><strong>Save weather records</strong> for each storm date as soon as they are available.</li>
</ul>

<p>If an insurer does say the damage is old or unrelated, our article on <a href="/insurance-company-disputes-storm-damage/">what to do when an insurance company disputes storm damage</a> walks through the common responses.</p>

<h2>What to do after a hailstorm in McAllen</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Stay safe.</strong> Do not climb onto a wet or damaged roof. Look from the ground and from windows first.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph hailstones</strong> next to a ruler, coin, or other object for scale, and note the time.</li>
<li><strong>Check collateral damage:</strong> gutters, downspouts, AC fins, vents, window screens, fences, cars, and outdoor furniture.</li>
<li><strong>Look inside</strong> for new ceiling stains or leaks, especially after the next rain.</li>
<li><strong>Prevent further damage</strong> with tarps or temporary repairs, and keep the receipts.</li>
<li><strong>Look up the storm</strong> using our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm event lookup</a> or <a href="/weather-events/">recent weather events</a> page, and save what you find.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly.</strong> Waiting can make it harder to tie damage to a specific date, especially in a busy hail season.</li>
<li><strong>Get an independent inspection</strong> if you are unsure about the damage or the insurer's findings.</li>
</ol>

<p>For a full walk-through, see <a href="/document-hail-damage-for-an-insurance-claim/">how to document hail damage for an insurance claim</a> and our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a>. To understand what the weather records can and cannot prove, read <a href="/noaa-weather-records-document-property-damage-mcallen/">using NOAA weather records to document property damage</a>.</p>

